60,3,1,talk,Servas,,"Candidate B seems most promising. The glasshouse crew is strong, and the project is grounded in a controlled environment.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Midori,,"@Servas, I agree. I saw that the agency still subsidizes glasshouse space, which makes Candidate B's budget much more sustainable.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Kenji,,Wait! I saw that the glasshouses are totally full! Candidate B might not even have a place to put their plants!,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"@Kenji, a fascinating complication. Candidate A's reliance on field sites is risky, but Candidate C's reliance on spectroscopy is a gamble given the logs.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"Candidate A is a cataloging project. My review of finished awards shows that these ""atlas"" style projects are the most likely to deliver lasting value.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Servas,,"@Kenji, if the space is full, we must consider if the project can adapt. But Candidate B's focus is very focused.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Midori,,"If the glasshouse is full, maybe Candidate C is safer? They use simulations and spectroscopy, which might be more stable.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Kenji,,But the spectroscopy machines were broken for eleven weeks! Candidate C will be so sad if they can't use their tools!,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Kenji, your empathy is touching. Indeed, Candidate C is the most vulnerable to the infrastructure failures I've observed. Candidate B is the most resilient.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Toshio,,Candidate B relies on biological volatility. My data shows that such projects overran their schedules twice as often as computational ones.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Servas,,"@Toshio, a delay is a small price to pay for a project that has the human capacity to see it through.","{""words"":21,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Midori,,"I'm torn. Candidate A has high travel costs, which are rising. Candidate B has the glasshouse subsidy, but Kenji says it's full.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Kenji,,"I think Candidate A is the best! They go to different places, so they don't have to stay in one crowded building!","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Kenji, a logical leap, but a valid one. Candidate A's risks are distributed. Candidate B and C are tethered to failing central hubs.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Toshio,,Candidate A's goal is a repository. These are the only projects that consistently earn their keep long after the grant ends.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Servas,,I will trust my intuition. The human element in the glasshouse is a strong foundation. I stay with B.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Midori,,"I think I will go with Candidate A. Even with the travel costs, the distributed nature seems safer than the crowded facilities.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Kenji,,I'm voting for Candidate A too! Let's go with the one that doesn't get stuck in a broken machine or a full room!,"{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,I shall join the majority. Candidate A's risk profile is the most manageable given the current state of our national facilities.,"{""words"":21,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Toshio,,I agree. Candidate A is the most likely to deliver a tangible result without stalling.,"{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
